WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.512
hello-devops answers requests all day.

2
00:00:05.512 --> 00:00:06.901
A few of them fail.

3
00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:08.364
That always happens.

4
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:11.603
So at 3 a.m., how many failures are too many?

5
00:00:11.603 --> 00:00:13.581
When should a person wake up?

6
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:15.182
Do not aim for 100%.

7
00:00:15.182 --> 00:00:23.368
Users rarely notice the difference between high and extreme reliability, and each extra nine costs far more work.

8
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:28.223
First, decide what good means, seen from the user's side.

9
00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:31.504
hello-devops has one real job: the JSON at /.

10
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.721
A good event is a response to / that is not 5xx.

11
00:00:35.721 --> 00:00:38.677
A valid event is any response to /.

12
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:44.740
A 404 for a mistyped URL is the user's mistake, so it does not count against you.

13
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:47.578
Good events divided by valid events.

14
00:00:47.578 --> 00:00:51.325
That ratio is the SLI, the service level indicator.

15
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:59.642
An SLO, a service level objective, is a target for that ratio over a time window.

16
00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:05.843
For hello-devops: 99.9% of requests to / succeed, over the last 30 days.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.946
The rest is the error budget: 100% minus the SLO.

18
00:01:10.946 --> 00:01:13.369
For 99.9%, that is 0.1%.

19
00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:17.305
It is the failure the SLO still allows.

20
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.131
Make it real.

21
00:01:19.131 --> 00:01:22.816
Say hello-devops gets 200,000 requests a day.

22
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.321
Thirty days hold 6 million requests, and 0.1% of them, 6,000, may fail.

23
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:36.437
If everything fails at once, the budget is 43.2 minutes of full outage.

24
00:01:36.437 --> 00:01:40.168
0.1% of the 43,200 minutes in 30 days.

25
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:44.440
Now one bad deploy breaks everything for 20 minutes.

26
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.256
20 out of 43.2 minutes.

27
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.109
That one deploy spent almost half the month.

28
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:52.003
So a rollback has to take minutes, not an hour.

29
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.431
Developers want to ship, and operators want quiet nights.

30
00:01:56.431 --> 00:01:59.235
The budget gives both sides the same number.

31
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.268
While budget is left, you ship at normal speed and take some risks.

32
00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:10.279
When it is spent, the SRE book's answer is to pause risky releases until reliability is back.

33
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:16.120
At 3 a.m., the useful question is not how much budget is spent.

34
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.115
It is how fast it is going.

35
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:24.289
The burn rate says how fast you are spending it: the current error ratio, divided by the budget.

36
00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:27.265
Draw the budget over the 30 day window.

37
00:02:27.265 --> 00:02:29.345
Full at day zero, at the top.

38
00:02:29.345 --> 00:02:30.780
Empty at the bottom.

39
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:35.962
If 0.1% of requests fail, the error ratio equals the budget.

40
00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:37.638
That is burn rate 1.

41
00:02:37.638 --> 00:02:40.404
The budget lasts exactly 30 days.

42
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.310
At 0.6%, the burn rate is 6.

43
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.117
The budget is gone in 5 days.

44
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:49.320
At 1.44%, the burn rate is 14.4.

45
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.659
The budget is gone in about 2 days.

46
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.303
Burn rate beats a raw error count, because it is already scaled to your traffic and your target.

47
00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:02.864
But why the odd number 14.4?

48
00:03:02.864 --> 00:03:04.256
Work backwards.

49
00:03:04.256 --> 00:03:06.390
30 days have 720 hours.

50
00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:11.771
At burn rate 1, the budget is spread over all 720 of them.

51
00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:18.033
The rule is: page once a single hour has spent 2% of the budget.

52
00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:21.845
2% of 720 hours is 14.4 hours of budget.

53
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.257
Spend those 14.4 hours of budget in one hour, and that is burn rate 14.4.

54
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.914
These numbers come from Google's SRE Workbook.

55
00:03:31.914 --> 00:03:35.581
Page at 14.4 over one hour, or 6 over six hours.

56
00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:38.255
Open a ticket at 1 over three days.

57
00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:42.284
Now sort every alert into one of three groups.

58
00:03:42.284 --> 00:03:45.502
Page: users hurt now, and a human must act now.

59
00:03:45.502 --> 00:03:47.831
Ticket: a human, in working hours.

60
00:03:47.831 --> 00:03:50.296
Everything else goes on a dashboard.

61
00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:55.513
A bad hour: 2% of requests fail for 60 minutes.

62
00:03:55.513 --> 00:04:01.097
The burn rate is 0.02 divided by 0.001, which is 20.

63
00:04:01.097 --> 00:04:03.137
That is above 14.4.

64
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:04.940
So this one pages.

65
00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:08.675
A slow leak: 0.3% of requests fail all week.

66
00:04:08.675 --> 00:04:12.699
That is burn rate 3, and the budget lasts 10 days.

67
00:04:12.867 --> 00:04:16.950
Nobody needs to wake up, but someone must fix it this week.

68
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:17.573
A ticket.

69
00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:23.142
And a server at 90% CPU that answers every request in 50 ms?

70
00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:25.576
It is simply doing its job.

71
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:30.926
High CPU, memory, a single pod restart: these are causes, not symptoms.

72
00:04:30.926 --> 00:04:33.120
Users never feel most of them.

73
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.948
They go on the dashboard.

74
00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:38.282
Page on causes, and the pager fires all the time.

75
00:04:38.282 --> 00:04:43.680
People learn that pages are noise, and start to ignore them, including the real one.

76
00:04:43.867 --> 00:04:50.060
An SLO turns raw requests into a budget, and the burn rate decides who gets woken up.
